# Clipboard bridge — forward the Mac clipboard channels between hosts so nvim /
# the pickers on a remote can read this Mac's clipboard and mirror its history.
# Pulled in by ~/.ssh/config via `Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*`, like personal.config.
#
# Both endpoints are LOOPBACK TCP PORTS, not unix sockets, and that choice is
# load-bearing: macOS's OpenSSH silently ignores `StreamLocalBindUnlink` for
# remote (`-R`) unix-socket forwards (verified — "remote port forwarding failed",
# socket left stale). So any unclean disconnect (sleep, network drop, mux master
# aging out) stranded a socket file that killed the reverse forward for EVERY
# later session until it was manually removed — the bridge "kind of worked" via
# OSC-52/cache fallbacks. A forwarded TCP port is owned by the ssh process and
# freed by the OS the instant the session ends, so there is nothing to go stale;
# a fresh (or multiplexed) session always rebinds cleanly. No keeper, no second
# connection — this rides whatever SSH path already reaches the mac-mini.
#
#   RemoteForward 127.0.0.1:2490 <- this Mac's clipboard-bridge on :2489
#     The remote gets a loopback listener on :2490 that tunnels back here; the
#     remote's nvim provider + pick-clipboard read the remote clipboard there.
#   LocalForward  127.0.0.1:2492 -> the remote's clipboard-mirror on :2491
#     This Mac binds :2492; the Hammerspoon capture watcher pushes each local
#     clip's text+metadata (no blobs) there, landing as a `remote-ref` row in
#     the remote's history. LocalForward connects to the remote's already-
#     listening [clipboard-mirror] service (unlike RemoteForward, which creates
#     its own endpoint).
#
# Loopback-only (127.0.0.1) so nothing off-host can reach either port. On a
# single-user personal Mac a loopback port is owner-equivalent to the old 0600
# socket; a shared host would want a protocol token instead (future).
#
# Host-specific blocks for non-public/work remotes are intentionally NOT kept
# here. They live in a loose, unmanaged ~/.ssh/config.d/ file that chezmoi
# never tracks, per the local-only SSH policy documented in config.example.
# These forwards are additive, so they layer cleanly on top of any other Host
# definition (e.g. a work-network ProxyJump) for the same alias.

Host thiago-mac-mini.local
  RemoteForward 127.0.0.1:2490 127.0.0.1:2489
  LocalForward  127.0.0.1:2492 127.0.0.1:2491
